## Proof

**Claim:** Yes, such a function exists for every $p \ge 1$, every $x_0 \in \mathbb{R}$, and every open set $I$ containing $x_0$.

**Construction.** Since $I$ is open and $x_0 \in I$, there exists $\varepsilon > 0$ such that the closed interval $[x_0 - \varepsilon,\, x_0 + \varepsilon] \subset I$. We construct a $C^\infty$ (hence $C^p$) function supported in $(x_0-\varepsilon, x_0+\varepsilon) \subseteq I$ that equals $1$ at $x_0$ and takes values in $[0,1]$.

Define the standard smooth bump on $(-1,1)$:
$$\psi(t) = \begin{cases} \exp\!\left(-\dfrac{1}{1-t^2}\right), & |t| < 1, \\[6pt] 0, & |t| \ge 1. \end{cases}$$

It is well known (and verified below) that $\psi \in C^\infty(\mathbb{R})$, $\psi \ge 0$, $\operatorname{supp}(\psi) = [-1,1]$, and $\psi(0) = e^{-1} > 0$. Now set
$$f(x) \;=\; \frac{\psi\!\big(\tfrac{x-x_0}{\varepsilon}\big)}{\psi(0)} \;=\; e\cdot \psi\!\Big(\tfrac{x-x_0}{\varepsilon}\Big).$$

**Verification of the required properties.**

1. **Support.** $\psi\!\big(\tfrac{x-x_0}{\varepsilon}\big) \neq 0$ only when $\big|\tfrac{x-x_0}{\varepsilon}\big| < 1$, i.e. $x \in (x_0-\varepsilon, x_0+\varepsilon)$. Hence $\operatorname{supp}(f) \subseteq [x_0-\varepsilon, x_0+\varepsilon] \subset I$.

2. **Value at $x_0$.** $f(x_0) = \dfrac{\psi(0)}{\psi(0)} = 1$.

3. **Sup-norm bound.** The function $g(t) = -\frac{1}{1-t^2}$ is strictly decreasing on $(-1,0]$ (its derivative $g'(t) = -\frac{2t}{(1-t^2)^2} \le 0$ there) and strictly increasing on $[0,1)$, so $g$ attains its maximum at $t=0$, where $g(0)=-1$. Thus $\psi(t) = e^{g(t)} \le e^{-1} = \psi(0)$ for all $t$, with equality only at $t=0$. Therefore $0 \le f(x) \le 1$ for all $x$, giving $\sup_{x\in\mathbb{R}} |f(x)| = 1 \le 1$.

4. **Regularity and vanishing at infinity.** The function $\psi$ is $C^\infty$: outside $(-1,1)$ it is identically $0$, and at the boundary points $t = \pm 1$ every derivative tends to $0$ (since for any $k$, $\psi^{(k)}(t) \to 0$ as $t \to 1^-$ — this follows because $\psi^{(k)}$ is a finite sum of terms of the form $P(1/(1-t^2))\,(1-t^2)^{-m}\,e^{-1/(1-t^2)}$ with $P$ a polynomial, and the exponential decay dominates every polynomial blow-up). Hence $\psi \in C^\infty(\mathbb{R})$, so $f \in C^\infty(\mathbb{R}) \subseteq C^p(\mathbb{R})$. Since $\operatorname{supp}(f)$ is compact, $f$ and all its derivatives vanish at infinity. Thus $f \in C_0^p(\mathbb{R})$.

**Conclusion.** For every $p \ge 1$, every $x_0 \in \mathbb{R}$, and every open neighborhood $I$ of $x_0$, there exists $f \in C_0^p(\mathbb{R})$ with $\operatorname{supp}(f) \subseteq I$, $f(x_0)=1$, and $\sup_{x\in\mathbb{R}}|f(x)| \le 1$. The key ingredient is the existence of smooth compactly supported "bump" functions, which are automatically members of $C_0^p(\mathbb{R})$ for every finite $p$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
